"comparison": "Both Goldman Sachs and Bank of America disclose that regulatory and legal restrictions limit the efficient movement of capital and liquidity among affiliates, thereby increasing consolidated requirements. Goldman Sachs explicitly cites 'ring fencing' and the inability to 'efficiently move capital and liquidity among our affiliated entities' as a result of government steps to protect local creditors (gs_2024_1A_P055_S002, gs_2024_1A_P055_S003). Similarly, Bank of America states there are 'legal, regulatory, contractual and other limitations on our ability to utilize liquidity from one legal entity to satisfy the liquidity requirements of another' (bac_2024_1A_P026_S003) and that bank/broker-dealer subsidiaries face restrictions on using funds to fund their businesses or transact with affiliates (bac_2024_1A_P027_S002).",
